1
00:00:01,313 --> 00:00:09,825
Let’s start from a fresh clone. mise is already installed and activated.

2
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:13,286
I’ve reviewed this project’s config. Now I tell mise I trust it.

3
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:19,954
I already have these tool versions. mise reuses the installations across projects.

4
00:00:19,954 --> 00:00:29,105
Here’s mise.toml. The tool versions and settings are right here.

5
00:00:29,105 --> 00:00:38,411
A little further down: the setup hook, and commands to test and run the app.

6
00:00:38,411 --> 00:00:42,412
Tools, settings, setup and shared commands. Python dependencies live in pyproject.toml and uv.lock.

7
00:00:42,412 --> 00:00:47,660
The setup hook syncs this project’s .venv. The greeting comes from mise.toml too.

8
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:51,164
Here in the root: Python 3.14.

9
00:00:51,164 --> 00:00:53,643
Now for the other Python.

10
00:00:53,643 --> 00:01:02,118
This folder inherits the other tools. Its own mise.toml overrides Python.

11
00:01:02,118 --> 00:01:05,619
Python 3.13 in this folder.

12
00:01:05,619 --> 00:01:07,649
Back to the root…

13
00:01:07,649 --> 00:01:11,579
…and back to Python 3.14.

14
00:01:11,579 --> 00:01:19,124
Setup done. Let’s see if it actually works.

15
00:01:19,124 --> 00:01:25,349
Tests pass. Let’s run the app.

16
00:01:25,349 --> 00:01:30,353
There we go. The app is running. Open the URL and we’re ready to code.
